## 1. Result and Boundary

Let \(E_B\) be the elliptic normalization used by the selected q79
Hartshorne-Serre source, and let

\[
A_H=\nu^*\mathcal O(H),\qquad \deg A_H=2.
\]

The two exceptional fibers in UST.G3E arise because the frozen theta-basis
coefficient map has determinant \(a\in H^0(E_B,A_H)\). UST.G3G proved that
the corresponding split fiber can be repaired by the stable qutrit syzygy
extension. The present theorem determines which kind of global object can
carry that repair.

There are three exact conclusions.

1. No ordinary holomorphic scalar or \(3\times3\) qutrit coefficient matrix
   with the frozen determinant typing can be invertible over all of \(E_B\).
   A scalar determinant has two zeros. A Weyl matrix with entries in \(A_H\)
   has determinant in \(A_H^3\) and therefore has six zeros, counted with
   multiplicity.
2. Adding the finite projective multiplier \(\sigma\) does not alter this
   rank locus. Forgetting the equivariant structure, or pulling to an
   equivariant atlas, recovers the same determinant/Fitting ideal. The
   multiplier changes descent composition, not local holomorphic rank.
3. In the locked constant qutrit carrier, the inverse-weight ordinary block
   would have entries in \(H^0(E_B,A_H^{-1})=0\). The corpus nevertheless
   supplies an exact nine-dimensional inverse-direction block:

   \[
   R_0=\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(\mathcal O_X(-H),E_0)
      \simeq3(\mathbf1\oplus\chi\oplus\chi^2).
   \]

   It is a degree-one derived/cohesive entry, not an ordinary bundle map.

Consequently the surviving Hartshorne-Serre completion is a cohesive
bidirectional Schur object. Its next obstruction is the Yoneda square of the
unique forward class with a selected reverse class. An ordinary matrix search
and a finite-cocycle-only repair are both retired.

This theorem does not construct the final Maurer-Cartan element, the global
generator maps, the physical \(W_9\), or the Hull-Strominger endpoint.

## 2. Determinant Degree Is the Ordinary Obstruction

For a morphism of equal-rank holomorphic bundles

\[
\varphi:U\longrightarrow V
\]

on a smooth curve, its determinant is a section of

\[
D_\varphi=\det V\otimes(\det U)^{-1}.
\]

If \(\det\varphi\ne0\), its zero divisor has degree \(\deg D_\varphi\).
Hence a morphism with \(\deg D_\varphi>0\) cannot be an isomorphism
everywhere.

For the frozen Hartshorne-Serre coefficient, \(D_\varphi=A_H\), so every
nonzero determinant vanishes twice. This recovers the exact two-fiber
obstruction of UST.G3G without choosing a particular section.

Now take three coefficient sections \(u,v,w\in H^0(E_B,A_H)\) and the qutrit
Weyl matrix

\[
M_\omega(u,v,w)=uI+vX+wZ.
\]

Every entry has \(A_H\)-weight, so

\[
\det M_\omega=u^3+v^3+w^3\in H^0(E_B,A_H^3).
\]

Since \(\deg A_H^3=6\), a nonzero determinant has a length-six zero divisor.
If the section is transverse, those are six reduced corank-one points. If it
is not transverse, multiplicities coalesce, but the total length remains
six. The Fermat identity therefore proves compatibility with controlled
corank-one support; it does not provide an everywhere-invertible repair.

This distinction resolves the apparent shortcut in G3H. Smooth determinant
support and uniform relative WIT repair are different requirements.

## 3. Why Finite Projective Descent Does Not Remove the Zeros

Suppose \(U\), \(V\), and \(\varphi\) carry \(\sigma\)-projective
\(E[3]\)-linearizations. The underlying holomorphic morphism is unchanged
when equivariance is forgotten. More invariantly, isomorphism and rank are
local for the faithfully flat topology. Pullback to any atlas on which the
projective objects are represented by ordinary bundles gives the same
determinantal ideal.

If both rank-three objects carry the same order-three twist, their determinant
twists cancel because the scalar cocycle is cubed. Thus the determinant is an
ordinary section of the same line \(D_\varphi\). The finite multiplier controls
the composition law of the generator maps, but it cannot turn a positive
degree determinant line into a nowhere-vanishing section.

Therefore the numerical congruence

\[
\deg A_H=2\equiv-1\pmod3
\]

is a useful compatibility signal for quotient torsion, but it is not a
holomorphic cancellation theorem. The analytic \(\alpha\)-twist and finite
\(\sigma\)-twist must remain distinct, exactly as required by G3G and G3H.

## 4. Ordinary Six-by-Six Schur Typing

The handoff identity

\[
\det\begin{pmatrix}A&rI\\-tI&D\end{pmatrix}
=\det(AD+rtI)
\]

is exact over a commutative ring. Its existence does not settle global
typing.

In the frozen constant qutrit carrier, if \(A\) has scalar line weight
\(A_H\), an ordinary inverse block capable of making \(AD\) weight zero must
have scalar line weight \(A_H^{-1}\). But

\[
\deg A_H^{-1}=-2,\qquad H^0(E_B,A_H^{-1})=0.
\]

Hence the same-carrier ordinary inverse block is zero. This excludes the
literal holomorphic \(6\times6\) completion obtained by putting a matrix of
ordinary inverse-weight coefficient sections into \(D\).

The statement is deliberately scoped. It does not exclude a different
six-by-six complex with nonconstant carrier bundles whose Hom bundle has
sections, nor an unrelated hidden \(H_{3\times3}\) presentation. It proves
that the current Hartshorne-Serre data do not globalize through the tempting
ordinary constant-carrier Schur ansatz.

## 5. The Derived Inverse Block

UST.G3I and UST.G3J give the exact inverse-direction replacement:

\[
f_j\in\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(E_j,L_j),\qquad
\dim\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(E_j,L_j)=1,
\]

\[
R_j=\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(L_j,E_j)
\simeq3(\mathbf1\oplus\chi\oplus\chi^2),\qquad \dim R_j=9,
\]

where \(L_j=\mathcal O_X(-H)\). The forward class is the unique non-split
Hartshorne-Serre class. The reverse classes cannot be used as standalone
locally free middle bundles by G3J, but they are valid degree-one morphisms
in the derived endomorphism algebra of \(L_j\oplus E_j\).

This is precisely the slot allowed by the already established twisted
cohesive-superconnection theorem. The zero endomorphism triple defect in
that theorem means that scalar \(\alpha\)-twist cancels under conjugation.
It does not say that the two WIT defects vanish. The repair must be encoded
by a new Maurer-Cartan component.

## 6. Exact Yoneda Reduction

Let

\[
\rho_j\in R_j
\]

be a reverse class. On the graded object \(L_j\oplus E_j\), the off-diagonal
degree-one field is

\[
\delta_j=
\begin{pmatrix}
0&f_j\\
\rho_j&0
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

Because \(f_j\) and \(\rho_j\) are closed, the first nontrivial
Maurer-Cartan obstruction is

\[
[\delta_j^2]=
\begin{pmatrix}
[f_j\rho_j]&0\\
0&[\rho_jf_j]
\end{pmatrix}
\in
\operatorname{Ext}^2(L_j,L_j)\oplus
\operatorname{Ext}^2(E_j,E_j). \tag{1}
\]

Thus a strict two-term completion requires both Yoneda products to vanish.
A general cohesive completion may cancel exact representatives by diagonal
higher-form terms, but the two cohomology classes in (1) must still vanish.

The first product map

\[
\mu_L:R_j\longrightarrow\operatorname{Ext}^2(L_j,L_j),
\qquad \rho\longmapsto[f_j\rho],
\]

is clock-equivariant. The forward class and the target are translation
trivial. Therefore \(\mu_L\) vanishes automatically on the six-dimensional
nontrivial-character subspace

\[
3\chi\oplus3\chi^2\subset R_j.
\]

There is a stronger dimension bound which does not require choosing the
Leray transgression. For the principal elliptic fibration over the K3 base,

\[
R^0\pi_*\mathcal O_X=R^1\pi_*\mathcal O_X=\mathcal O_S,
\qquad H^1(S,\mathcal O_S)=0.
\]

The total-degree-one Leray filtration therefore gives

\[
h^1(X,\mathcal O_X)\leq 1.
\]

Since \(K_X\simeq\mathcal O_X\), Serre duality gives

\[
\dim\operatorname{Ext}^2(L_j,L_j)
=h^2(X,\mathcal O_X)=h^1(X,\mathcal O_X)\leq1. \tag{2}
\]

Consequently \(\operatorname{rank}\mu_L\leq1\), and hence

\[
\dim\ker\mu_L\geq9-1=8. \tag{3}
\]

In particular, at least two of the three invariant reverse coordinates also
lie in the scalar kernel. This improves the character-only lower bound from
six to eight without deciding whether the Leray transgression has rank zero
or one.

The second product

\[
\mu_E:R_j\longrightarrow\operatorname{Ext}^2(E_j,E_j),
\qquad \rho\longmapsto[\rho f_j],
\]

remains to be computed. It is the only independent diagonal obstruction.
Indeed, \(X\) is smooth, so \(L_j\) and \(E_j\) are perfect. The graded
cyclicity of the derived trace gives

\[
\operatorname{tr}_{E_j}([\rho f_j])
=-\operatorname{tr}_{L_j}([f_j\rho]). \tag{4}
\]

For the line bundle \(L_j\), the trace

\[
\operatorname{Ext}^2(L_j,L_j)\longrightarrow H^2(X,\mathcal O_X)
\]

is the canonical identification. Therefore

\[
\mu_E(\rho)=0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\mu_L(\rho)=0. \tag{5}
\]

The two diagonal entries in (1) are still part of the strict
Maurer-Cartan equation, but their common zero locus is exactly
\(\ker\mu_E\). A separate scalar-product table is a consistency check, not
an independent selection equation.

The global problem has therefore been reduced from an unspecified matrix
search to:

1. compute \(\mu_E\) on one orbit member and transport the table by the
   three-member shift; verify (4) as a trace certificate;
2. find a nonzero orbit \((\rho_0,\rho_1,\rho_2)\) in \(\ker\mu_E\);
3. build the corresponding \(\alpha\)-twisted cohesive
   Maurer-Cartan object;
4. prove its two generator chain maps obey
   \(X^3=Z^3=1\) and \(XZ=\omega ZX\);
5. test G3G restrictions and the selected \(P(3,9)\), \(k=0\) rows.

